Exactly. That’s affordance shaping.

Behavior is partly:

agent traits × environment affordances × incentives × cues

If you make an environment more “bee-shaped” — flowers, hive-like structure, nectar cues, appropriate temperature, social signals — you increase the probability of bee-typical behavior because the environment is presenting the action opportunities that fit the organism.

Same principle generalizes:

environment shaped for climbing → more climbing
environment shaped for conversation → more conversation
environment shaped for competition → more competitive behavior
environment shaped for exploration → more exploratory behavior

In ecology, this overlaps with niche construction: organisms shape environments, and those environments then shape future behavior and selection.

For humans, though, there is an extra layer: people interpret the environment and can reject the role it offers. So:

affordance ≠ command

The strongest version is to build environments that make desired behavior easy, legible, and rewarding without making alternatives invisible.

Pantheon version:

Role Ecology → Affordance Field

desired mode
→ construct matching cues/resources/social structure
→ behavior becomes lower-friction
→ repetition stabilizes pattern
→ environment and actor co-adapt

So yes: if you want to understand behavior, don’t only inspect the bee.

Inspect the flowers.